th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-2/1a-Math-V-Advanced-Linear-Algebra/kronecker-product.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/1a-Math-V-Advanced-Linear-Algebra/kronecker-product.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For an $m\\times n$ matrix $A=(a_{ij})$ and a $p\\times q$ matrix $B=(b_{kl})$, the Kronecker product $A\\otimes B$ is a block matrix with $m$ block rows and $n$ block columns. Which block sits in block row $i$, block column $j$?","options":["$b_{ij}A$, an $m\\times n$ block","$a_{ji}B$, a $p\\times q$ block","$a_{ij}b_{ij}$, a single number","$a_{ij}B$, a $p\\times q$ block"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0f672bc8caf78f201a8c0040","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a $2\\times3$ matrix $A$ and a $4\\times2$ matrix $B$, how do the Kronecker products $A\\otimes B$ and $B\\otimes A$ compare?","options":["They are always equal, since the Kronecker product is commutative","They have different sizes, $8\\times6$ and $6\\times8$ respectively","Both are $8\\times6$ with the same entries, arranged differently in general","$B\\otimes A$ is always the transpose of $A\\otimes B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4c4cd37d68237d0940785bf3","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A$ be a $2\\times3$ matrix and $B$ a $4\\times5$ matrix. Which of the ordinary product $AB$ and the Kronecker product $A\\otimes B$ is defined, and what is its size?","options":["Only $A\\otimes B$, which is $6\\times20$","Both, with $AB$ of size $2\\times5$","Neither, as the inner sizes $3$ and $4$ differ","Only $A\\otimes B$, which is $8\\times15$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8ed1effae9884566de0c00aa","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A,B,C,D$ be matrices such that the ordinary products $AC$ and $BD$ are defined. Which identity for Kronecker products holds?","options":["$(A\\otimes B)(C\\otimes D)=(AC)\\otimes(BD)$","$(A\\otimes B)(C\\otimes D)=(AD)\\otimes(BC)$","$(A\\otimes B)(C\\otimes D)=(CA)\\otimes(DB)$","$(A\\otimes B)(C\\otimes D)=(A\\otimes C)(B\\otimes D)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"25689b21fd70495051593300","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A$ ($n\\times n$) and $B$ ($p\\times p$) be invertible. Which matrix is the inverse of the Kronecker product $A\\otimes B$?","options":["$A^{-1}\\otimes B^{-1}$","$B^{-1}\\otimes A^{-1}$","$A^{-1}\\otimes B$","$A^{\\top}\\otimes B^{\\top}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9d912064d1ab2671bca4910a","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For the column vectors $\\boldsymbol{x}=(2,-1)^{\\top}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}=(1,3)^{\\top}$, what is the Kronecker product $\\boldsymbol{x}\\otimes\\boldsymbol{y}$?","options":["$(2,-1,6,-3)^{\\top}$","$(2,6,-1,-3)^{\\top}$","$(2,-3)^{\\top}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&6\\\\-1&-3\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a9734b5225b4c60f1b0a2898","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{pmatrix}1&4\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}$. What is the trace of the $4\\times4$ Kronecker product $A\\otimes B$?","options":["$7$","$100$","$5$","$10$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c049240e7ba0821d75ea23bb","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A$ be a $2\\times2$ matrix with $\\det A=3$ and $B$ a $3\\times3$ matrix with $\\det B=2$. What is the determinant of the $6\\times6$ matrix $A\\otimes B$?","options":["$6$","$72$","$36$","$108$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f0df255e4d5054c9cca08e84","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $B=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$ and let $I_2$ be the $2\\times2$ identity matrix. Which matrix is the Kronecker product $I_2\\otimes B$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&0&0\\\\3&4&0&0\\\\0&0&1&2\\\\0&0&3&4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&0&2&0\\\\0&1&0&2\\\\3&0&4&0\\\\0&3&0&4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&1&2\\\\3&4&3&4\\\\1&2&1&2\\\\3&4&3&4\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"67c9a3011b9722523dab71c1","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A$ ($n\\times n$) satisfy $A\\boldsymbol{x}=\\lambda\\boldsymbol{x}$ and $B$ ($p\\times p$) satisfy $B\\boldsymbol{y}=\\mu\\boldsymbol{y}$, with $\\boldsymbol{x},\\boldsymbol{y}\\neq\\boldsymbol{0}$. Which vector is an eigenvector of $A\\otimes B$, and for which eigenvalue?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{x}\\otimes\\boldsymbol{y}$, for the eigenvalue $\\lambda+\\mu$","$\\boldsymbol{y}\\otimes\\boldsymbol{x}$, for the eigenvalue $\\lambda\\mu$","$\\boldsymbol{x}\\otimes\\boldsymbol{y}$, for the eigenvalue $\\lambda\\mu$","$\\boldsymbol{x}\\otimes\\boldsymbol{y}$, for the eigenvalue $\\lambda^p\\mu^n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0d87eacfac7430015cd2670c","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A,A'$ be matrices of one size, $B,B'$ matrices of another size, $C$ any matrix and $c$ a scalar. Which identity for Kronecker products holds in general?","options":["$(A+A')\\otimes(B+B')=A\\otimes B+A'\\otimes B'$","$(A+A')\\otimes B=A\\otimes B+A'\\otimes B$","$(cA)\\otimes(cB)=c\\,(A\\otimes B)$","$(A\\otimes B)\\otimes C=(A\\otimes C)\\otimes B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d78d0ac77be116805e021750","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For an $m\\times n$ matrix $A=(a_{ij})$ and a $p\\times q$ matrix $B$, which matrix equals the transpose $(A\\otimes B)^{\\top}$?","options":["$B^{\\top}\\otimes A^{\\top}$","$A^{\\top}\\otimes B$","$A^{\\top}\\otimes B^{\\top}$","$B\\otimes A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"97abc08be5027b72c6e91997","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=(a_{ij})$ be $2\\times2$ and $B=(b_{kl})$ be $3\\times3$. Which product is the entry in row $5$, column $3$ of the $6\\times6$ Kronecker product $A\\otimes B$?","options":["$a_{11}b_{32}$","$a_{12}b_{32}$","$a_{21}b_{23}$","$a_{21}b_{32}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dec2cd0c134b3413115b8a3a","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Compute $\\operatorname{rank}(A\\otimes B)$, where $A\\otimes B$ is the $6\\times6$ Kronecker product of the $2\\times3$ matrix $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\\\2&4&6\\end{pmatrix}$ with the $3\\times2$ matrix $B=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\2&4\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$.","options":["$3$","$2$","$1$","$6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"481ff78611c1ea7f816fe79c","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&2\\end{pmatrix}$, $B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\boldsymbol{x}=(1,1)^{\\top}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}=(3,-1)^{\\top}$. What is the vector $(A\\otimes B)(\\boldsymbol{x}\\otimes\\boldsymbol{y})$?","options":["$(-2,6,-2,6)^{\\top}$","$(-2,-2,6,6)^{\\top}$","$(6,-2,6,-2)^{\\top}$","$(-1,3,-1,3)^{\\top}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"79e678d148199653214ddf05","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For two $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ and $B$ with $n\\ge2$, which condition guarantees that the Kronecker products agree, $A\\otimes B=B\\otimes A$?","options":["$A$ and $B$ commute: $AB=BA$","$B=cA$ for some scalar $c$","$A$ and $B$ are both diagonal matrices","$A$ and $B$ are both symmetric matrices"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d52b7481a4912df3b671c5f6","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$. The $4\\times4$ system $(A\\otimes B)\\boldsymbol{z}=(15,6,10,4)^{\\top}$ has right-hand side $\\boldsymbol{x}\\otimes\\boldsymbol{y}$ with $\\boldsymbol{x}=(3,2)^{\\top}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}=(5,2)^{\\top}$. What is its solution $\\boldsymbol{z}$?","options":["$(1,2,1,2)^{\\top}$","$(1,1,2,2)^{\\top}$","$(5,2,5,2)^{\\top}$","$(3,6,2,4)^{\\top}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a2d0a7599c5aae1aa10e50f3","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_018_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix and $B$ a $p\\times p$ matrix. Which condition is necessary and sufficient for the $np\\times np$ Kronecker product $A\\otimes B$ to be invertible?","options":["At least one of $A$ and $B$ is invertible","Both $A$ and $B$ are invertible","$A$ is invertible; $B$ only fills the blocks","Both $A$ and $B$ are invertible and $n=p$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"83723a0f8a7d4883accede5d","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_kronecker_product_48908698_020_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A $2\\times2$ matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $2$ and $-1$. Counted with multiplicity, what are the eigenvalues of the $4\\times4$ Kronecker product $A\\otimes A$?","options":["$4,\\ 4,\\ 1,\\ 1$","$4,\\ 1,\\ 1,\\ -2$","$2,\\ -1,\\ 2,\\ -1$","$4,\\ -2,\\ -2,\\ 1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"258c526aba0bb2f430150285","time_limit":30}]}}